{"name":"Demon","hex":"#7a0006","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Demon","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of demon (“card game”)."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A male given name from Ancient Greek."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Demon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Demon","blocks":[{"text":"A demon is an evil or malevolent supernatural entity, though they can be benevolent too. Historically, belief in demons, or stories about demons, occurs in folklore, mythology, religion, occultism, and literature; these beliefs are reflected in media including fiction, comics, film, television, and video games. Belief in demons probably goes back to the Paleolithic age, stemming from humanity's fear of the unknown, the strange and the horrific. In ancient Near Eastern religions and in the Abrahamic religions, including early Judaism and ancient-medieval Christian demonology, a demon is considered a harmful spiritual entity that may cause demonic possession, calling for an exorcism. Large portions of Jewish demonology, a key influence on Christianity and Islam, originated from a later form of Zoroastrianism, and was transferred to Judaism during the Persian era."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/PazuzuDemonAssyria1stMillenniumBCE_no_CGI_dropshadow_-_gradient_based_on.jpg/330px-PazuzuDemonAssyria1stMillenniumBCE_no_CGI_dropshadow_-_gradient_based_on.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demon","wikidata":"Q177413"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Mephistopheles2.jpg/500px-Mephistopheles2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Mephistopheles2.jpg/1280px-Mephistopheles2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src"